================================================================================
   COMPLETE GUIDE: CONNECTING ONE DESKTOP TO 2 WI-FI NETWORKS FOR ROS 2
================================================================================

YOUR CONFIRMED SETUP SUMMARY:
  drone_a (Tello)  →  IP: 192.168.10.2    gateway: 192.168.10.1   middleware: CycloneDDS
  drone_b (Astro)  →  IP: 192.168.8.163   gateway: 192.168.8.1    middleware: Zenoh
  Astro drone IP   →  192.168.8.24        Zenoh port: 7447         ASTRO_NUM: 4


================================================================================
PART 1 — UNDERSTANDING THE SETUP
================================================================================

    Your Desktop PC
    ├── Wi-Fi Adapter #1 (built-in, drone_a) ──────► 192.168.10.x  (Tello drone)
    └── Wi-Fi Adapter #2 (USB dongle, drone_b) ────► 192.168.8.x   (Astro drone)

Each adapter uses a different ROS 2 middleware:
  - drone_a (Tello) → CycloneDDS  (rmw_cyclonedds_cpp)  — no XML config needed
  - drone_b (Astro) → Zenoh       (rmw_zenoh_cpp)        — configured via env vars

You need TWO physical Wi-Fi adapters:
  - One built-in + one USB dongle, OR
  - Two USB dongles

One adapter cannot reliably connect to two networks simultaneously.


================================================================================
PART 2 — HARDWARE REQUIREMENTS
================================================================================

What you need:
  [OK] Your desktop PC (with one built-in Wi-Fi or a PCIe card)
  [OK] 1x USB Wi-Fi adapter (dongle) — e.g., TP-Link TL-WN823N or any dual-band
  [OK] Two separate Wi-Fi networks (Tello hotspot + Astro hotspot)

NOTE: If your desktop has NO built-in Wi-Fi, you need 2x USB Wi-Fi dongles.


================================================================================
PART 3 — LINUX SETUP (Ubuntu 22.04 / ROS 2 Humble assumed)
================================================================================

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1 — Plug in your USB Wi-Fi dongle
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Check that the system sees both adapters:

    ip link show

YOUR INTERFACES (already confirmed):
  - drone_a   (built-in, also known as wlp0s20f3 / wls5f3,  MAC: c8:58:c0:39:4e:05)
  - drone_b   (USB dongle,                                   MAC: 28:87:ba:78:6c:3c)

Confirm with:

    iwconfig

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 2 — Install NetworkManager tools
------------------------------------------------------------------------

    sudo apt update
    sudo apt install network-manager net-tools wireless-tools

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 3 — Connect each adapter to its own network
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Connect drone_a to the Tello network:

    nmcli device wifi connect "SSID_TELLO" password "password_tello" ifname drone_a

Connect drone_b to the Astro network:

    nmcli device wifi connect "SSID_ASTRO" password "password_astro" ifname drone_b

Replace SSID_TELLO, SSID_ASTRO, and passwords with your actual values.

Verify both are connected:

    nmcli device status

Both interfaces should show status: connected

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 4 — Check assigned IP addresses
------------------------------------------------------------------------

    ip addr show drone_a
    ip addr show drone_b

YOUR IPs (already confirmed):
  drone_a:  192.168.10.2    (network: 192.168.10.0/24,  gateway: 192.168.10.1)
  drone_b:  192.168.8.163   (network: 192.168.8.0/24,   gateway: 192.168.8.1)


================================================================================
PART 4 — ROUTING
================================================================================

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Do you need to set up routing tables? — Decision guide
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Run this first:

    ip route show

Then check your output against these two cases:

CASE A — Different subnets (your situation):
  You see separate entries like:
    192.168.10.0/24 dev drone_a ...
    192.168.8.0/24  dev drone_b ...
  → ROUTING TABLES NOT NEEDED. Linux already knows which interface
    to use for each network. Skip to the Verification section below.

CASE B — Same subnet on both interfaces:
  Both interfaces show the same range, e.g.:
    192.168.10.0/24 dev drone_a ...
    192.168.10.0/24 dev drone_b ...
  → ROUTING TABLES REQUIRED. Follow the steps in the
    "If routing tables are needed" section below.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
YOUR CONFIRMED ROUTING TABLE (Case A — no extra config needed)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

    default via 192.168.8.1  dev drone_b  metric 601     <- primary default (drone_b)
    default via 192.168.10.1 dev drone_a  metric 20602   <- fallback default (drone_a)
    192.168.8.0/24  dev drone_b  src 192.168.8.163       <- drone_b traffic
    192.168.10.0/24 dev drone_a  src 192.168.10.2        <- drone_a traffic

This means:
  - Traffic to 192.168.10.x automatically goes out through drone_a  [OK]
  - Traffic to 192.168.8.x  automatically goes out through drone_b  [OK]

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Verification — ping each device's gateway to confirm routing works
------------------------------------------------------------------------

    ping -I drone_a 192.168.10.1    # ping Tello gateway
    ping -I drone_b 192.168.8.1     # ping Astro gateway

If both reply, routing is working correctly. Proceed to Part 5.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
If routing tables are needed (Case B only)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Step 1 — Add separate routing tables:

    echo "100 net_a" | sudo tee -a /etc/iproute2/rt_tables
    echo "200 net_b" | sudo tee -a /etc/iproute2/rt_tables

Step 2 — Add routes to each table (replace IPs with yours):

    sudo ip route add 192.168.10.0/24 dev drone_a src 192.168.10.2 table net_a
    sudo ip route add default via 192.168.10.1 dev drone_a table net_a

    sudo ip route add 192.168.8.0/24 dev drone_b src 192.168.8.163 table net_b
    sudo ip route add default via 192.168.8.1 dev drone_b table net_b

Step 3 — Add rules to tell Linux which table to use:

    sudo ip rule add from 192.168.10.2 table net_a
    sudo ip rule add from 192.168.8.163 table net_b

Step 4 — Make persistent across reboots:

    sudo nano /etc/network/dual-wifi-routes.sh

    Paste this content:

        #!/bin/bash
        sleep 5
        ip route add 192.168.10.0/24 dev drone_a src 192.168.10.2 table net_a
        ip route add default via 192.168.10.1 dev drone_a table net_a
        ip route add 192.168.8.0/24 dev drone_b src 192.168.8.163 table net_b
        ip route add default via 192.168.8.1 dev drone_b table net_b
        ip rule add from 192.168.10.2 table net_a
        ip rule add from 192.168.8.163 table net_b

    Make it executable and add to crontab:

        sudo chmod +x /etc/network/dual-wifi-routes.sh
        sudo crontab -e
        # Add this line:
        @reboot /etc/network/dual-wifi-routes.sh

------------------------------------------------------------------------
IMPORTANT — drone_a IP lease warning
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Your drone_a IP (192.168.10.2) has a limited DHCP lease (~1 hour at setup time).
If it changes after reconnecting, CycloneDDS may bind to the wrong IP.

To fix: reserve the IP in your Tello's DHCP by binding MAC c8:58:c0:39:4e:05
to 192.168.10.2.


================================================================================
PART 5 — ROS 2 MIDDLEWARE CONFIGURATION
================================================================================

YOUR MIDDLEWARE SETUP:
  drone_a (Tello) → CycloneDDS  → rmw_cyclonedds_cpp  → Domain ID: 10
  drone_b (Astro) → Zenoh       → rmw_zenoh_cpp        → Domain ID: 4  (= ASTRO_NUM)

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Understanding the two middlewares
------------------------------------------------------------------------

CycloneDDS (drone_a / Tello):
  - Default DDS implementation, simple to configure
  - Automatically uses the correct interface as long as routing is correct
  - No XML config file needed — routing already handles it (Part 4)

Zenoh (drone_b / Astro):
  - Lighter, more efficient protocol used by Astro
  - Configured entirely via environment variables
  - Connects directly to the Astro drone's Zenoh router at 192.168.8.24:7447

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 1 — Install both middleware packages
------------------------------------------------------------------------

    sudo apt install ros-humble-rmw-cyclonedds-cpp
    sudo apt install ros-humble-rmw-zenoh-cpp

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 2 — Configure drone_a (Tello) — CycloneDDS
------------------------------------------------------------------------

No XML config file needed. Just set these two environment variables
before launching Tello nodes:

    export RMW_IMPLEMENTATION=rmw_cyclonedds_cpp
    export ROS_DOMAIN_ID=10

CycloneDDS will automatically use drone_a (192.168.10.x) because
the routing table already directs Tello traffic through that interface.

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 3 — Configure drone_b (Astro) — Zenoh
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Set these environment variables before launching Astro nodes:

    export RMW_IMPLEMENTATION=rmw_zenoh_cpp
    export ASTRO_NUM=4
    export ROS_DOMAIN_ID=$ASTRO_NUM
    export ZENOH_CONFIG_OVERRIDE="mode=\"client\";connect/endpoints=[\"tcp/192.168.8.2${ASTRO_NUM}:7447\"]"

What each variable does:
  RMW_IMPLEMENTATION    → tells ROS 2 to use Zenoh instead of DDS
  ASTRO_NUM             → your drone number (4), used to build the IP
  ROS_DOMAIN_ID         → set to 4 (= ASTRO_NUM), isolates Astro traffic
  ZENOH_CONFIG_OVERRIDE → connects to Astro at 192.168.8.24 port 7447
                          (8.2 + ASTRO_NUM = 8.24)

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Step 4 — Launch nodes for each drone
------------------------------------------------------------------------

Terminal 1 — Tello nodes (drone_a, 192.168.10.x):

    export RMW_IMPLEMENTATION=rmw_cyclonedds_cpp
    export ROS_DOMAIN_ID=10
    ros2 run your_package your_tello_node

Terminal 2 — Astro nodes (drone_b, 192.168.8.x):

    export RMW_IMPLEMENTATION=rmw_zenoh_cpp
    export ASTRO_NUM=4
    export ROS_DOMAIN_ID=$ASTRO_NUM
    export ZENOH_CONFIG_OVERRIDE="mode=\"client\";connect/endpoints=[\"tcp/192.168.8.2${ASTRO_NUM}:7447\"]"
    ros2 run your_package your_astro_node


================================================================================
PART 6 — SENDING DIFFERENT COMMANDS TO EACH DRONE
================================================================================

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Architecture
------------------------------------------------------------------------

                    +-------------------------+
                    |    Master Controller    |
                    |   (Your ROS 2 node)     |
                    +--------+--------+-------+
                             |        |
              +--------------+        +--------------+
              v                                       v
   /drone_a/cmd_vel                       /drone_b/cmd_vel
   Domain ID: 10                          Domain ID: 4
   CycloneDDS -> drone_a                  Zenoh -> drone_b
   192.168.10.2                           192.168.8.163 -> 192.168.8.24
              |                                       |
              v                                       v
        [Tello Drone]                          [Astro Drone]
       192.168.10.x                           192.168.8.24

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Example: commander node for Tello (dual_commander_tello.py)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

    import rclpy
    from rclpy.node import Node
    from geometry_msgs.msg import Twist

    class TelloCommander(Node):
        def __init__(self):
            super().__init__('tello_commander')
            # Run with: RMW_IMPLEMENTATION=rmw_cyclonedds_cpp ROS_DOMAIN_ID=10
            self.pub = self.create_publisher(Twist, '/drone_a/cmd_vel', 10)
            self.timer = self.create_timer(0.1, self.send_commands)

        def send_commands(self):
            cmd = Twist()
            cmd.linear.x = 0.5
            self.pub.publish(cmd)
            self.get_logger().info('Sent command to Tello (192.168.10.x)')

    def main():
        rclpy.init()
        rclpy.spin(TelloCommander())

    if __name__ == '__main__':
        main()

------------------------------------------------------------------------
Example: commander node for Astro (dual_commander_astro.py)
------------------------------------------------------------------------

    import rclpy
    from rclpy.node import Node
    from geometry_msgs.msg import Twist

    class AstroCommander(Node):
        def __init__(self):
            super().__init__('astro_commander')
            # Run with: RMW_IMPLEMENTATION=rmw_zenoh_cpp ROS_DOMAIN_ID=4
            #           ZENOH_CONFIG_OVERRIDE set as per Part 5 Step 3
            self.pub = self.create_publisher(Twist, '/drone_b/cmd_vel', 10)
            self.timer = self.create_timer(0.1, self.send_commands)

        def send_commands(self):
            cmd = Twist()
            cmd.linear.x = 0.5
            self.pub.publish(cmd)
            self.get_logger().info('Sent command to Astro (192.168.8.24)')

    def main():
        rclpy.init()
        rclpy.spin(AstroCommander())

    if __name__ == '__main__':
        main()


